## Commands

Always use `sbt --error …` to keep output short; drop `--error` only when you need the details.

| Task | Command |
|---|---|
{%- if dev_compose %}
| Start the dev database | `docker compose up -d` |
{%- endif %}
| Run tests | `sbt --error test`: incremental in sbt 2, so it only reruns tests whose code changed. `sbt --error testFull` runs everything |
| Run the server | `sbt server/run` (config: `server/src/main/resources/application.conf`, override with `-Dconfig.file=…`) |
| Regenerate the GraphQL schema file | `sbt server/calibanRender`, which writes `server/src/main/graphql/schema.graphql` |
{%- if components == 'full-stack' %}
| First-time / after changing `stLib/package.json` | `cd stLib && npm install && sbt --error publishLocal`, then bump `stlibVersion` in `build.sbt` (the first run takes several minutes and several GB of memory) |
| Build the client (dev) | `sbt client/webDebugDist`, served by the server from `debugDist/` |
| Build the client (prod) | `sbt client/webDist`, which writes `dist/` |
{%- endif %}
| Format | `sbt scalafmtAll` |
{%- if packaging == 'docker' %}
| Docker image | `sbt server/Docker/publishLocal` |
{%- elif packaging == 'systemd' %}
| Debian package | `sbt server/Debian/packageBin` |
{%- endif %}

## Architecture rules

- **One repository contract, two implementations.** `Repository[F[_]]` (in `model`) gathers every entity's
  operations. The server implements it with ZIO over the database (`type ZIORepository = Repository[DataIO]`,
  `DataIO[A] = IO[RepositoryError, A]`){% if components == 'full-stack' %}; the client implements it with `AsyncCallback` over GraphQL
  (`{{ class_prefix }}ClientRepository`){% endif %}. Operations never throw and never return `null`.
- **No pass-through layers.** GraphQL resolvers call the repository directly (`withRepository(_.modelObjectOps...)` in
  `{{ class_prefix }}Api`). Don't add service objects or accessor methods that only forward to a ZIO layer; add a
  service only when there's real logic that belongs in neither the API nor the repository.
- **Errors:** `RepositoryError` (`NotFound`, `Invalid`, `Conflict`, `Transient`, `Unexpected`). `{{ class_prefix }}Api`
  maps it to Caliban errors whose `extensions.code` is an `ErrorCode` (`BAD_USER_INPUT`, `NOT_FOUND`, `CONFLICT`,
  `UNAVAILABLE`, `INTERNAL`); unexpected errors are logged and reported without details.{% if components == 'full-stack' %} The client repository turns the
  codes back into the same `RepositoryError`s.{% endif %} Input validation lives in the API (`invalid(...)`).
- **Layers:** `server/.../AppLayers.scala` wires config → `DataSources.live` (pooled, migrated by Flyway, closed on
  shutdown) → `ZIORepository.live`. `AppLayers.mock` uses `MockRepository.live` instead, for tests.
- **Tracing:** OpenTelemetry through zio-telemetry. `Telemetry.live` builds a tracer from `app.telemetry`; with no
  endpoint configured it records nothing, so tracing costs nothing in tests and development. `{{ class_prefix }}Api`
  puts a span around each GraphQL operation; use `ZIO.serviceWithZIO[Tracing](_.span("name")(...))` for more.
{%- if ai == 'langchain4j' %}
- **AI:** `ai/src/main/scala/{{ pkg }}/ai/AiService.scala` is the service; `ChatModels` builds the configured
  provider's model, and `AiApi` is the GraphQL fragment that `ApiDefinition` combines with the application's API.
  With `app.ai.provider = none`, AI calls fail with a message saying so; tests use a stub model and never call a
  provider. Add AI features to `AiService`, and their mutations to `AiApi`.
{%- endif %}
{%- if auth == 'zio-auth' %}
- **Authentication:** `db/.../auth/UserStore.scala` holds the users and their password hashes (PBKDF2). It is plain
  JDBC rather than {{ db_layer }}, because the same few statements then work on every database; your own entities
  still belong in the repository. `server/.../AuthModule.scala` implements zio-auth's `AuthServer` over it, and
  `AuthRoutes.secure` mounts the login routes and puts the application's behind `bearerSessionProvider`. A route that
  must refuse anonymous callers asks for `Session[User, ConnectionId]` and matches on it; nothing does yet, so the
  GraphQL API is still open. Registration emails are logged, not sent — see `sendEmail` in `AuthModule`.
{%- endif %}
- **Tests:** each entity's operations pass `CRUDOperationsContract` (against the mock and the real database); HTTP
  behaviour is in `ServerContractSpec` (real HTTP, random port). Both use ZIO Test's *live* Clock and Random.
- **Schema changes only through new Flyway migrations** (`V<n>__description.sql` in
  `db/src/main/resources/db/migration/{{ migration_dir }}/`). Never edit a migration that has already run anywhere.
- **Timestamps are `java.time.Instant`.**{% if database == 'sqlite' %} SQLite stores them as `INTEGER` epoch
  milliseconds; declare timestamp columns `integer`.{% endif %}
- **Saves:** `upsert` inserts when the id is the entity's `empty` id, and updates otherwise.
- **Entities:** to add one, use the `add-entity` skill (`.claude/skills/add-entity/SKILL.md`). `ModelObject` is the
  reference implementation that every new entity mirrors.

## Compiler rules

- `-Werror`: every warning is an error. Fix the cause; add a narrowly scoped `-Wconf` only when the warning comes from
  code you don't control, with a comment saying why.
- `-Yexplicit-nulls`: Java APIs return `T | Null`. Handle the null, or add a local `import scala.language.unsafeNulls`
  in the smallest possible scope.
- ZIO's `Tag` (izumi-reflect) can't handle the "flexible" types of Java calls under explicit nulls. When a Java-returned
  value ends up in a `ZLayer` or `ZIO` type, give it an explicit Scala type (`val p: Path = Files.createTempDirectory(...).nn`).
- Caliban: `graphQL(...)` infers its environment as `Any` unless told otherwise. Keep the explicit
  `given Schema[ZIORepository, Queries]` / `Mutations` in `{{ class_prefix }}Api.scala`, and derive through its `ApiSchema` object.
{%- if http_server == 'http4s' %}
- http4s: every route runs in `ApiRoutes.F` (= `RIO[ZIORepository, *]`), because Caliban's routes need the
  repository. Build new routes in `F`, not `Task`, or they won't combine.
- fs2 3.7+: provide `given Files[F] = Files.forAsync[F]` and `given Network[F] = Network.forAsync[F]` explicitly. The
  implicit derivation from `Async` is deprecated, which fails the build.
{%- endif %}
{%- if db_layer == 'slick' %}
- Slick's `api.*` and `zio.*` both export `Tag`. In Slick files, import ZIO members selectively (`import zio.{RIO, ZIO}`).
- Slick: always `import SlickProfile.instantColumnType` by name next to `SlickProfile.profile.api.*`. Slick's own
  Instant mapping differs per profile (MySQL writes a VARCHAR), and the named import shadows it.
{%- endif %}
{%- if db_layer == 'doobie' %}
- doobie time mappings live in `db/src/main/scala/{{ pkg }}/db/DoobieMappings.scala`. Use its `Meta` instances,
  not `doobie.implicits.javatimedrivernative`{% if database == 'sqlite' %} (sqlite-jdbc can't read an `Instant` directly){% endif %}.
{%- endif %}
{%- if components == 'full-stack' %}

## Client rules

- Import MUI components as `import {{ base_package }}.st.muiMaterial.components.{List as MuiList, *}`. MUI has a
  `List` component that would otherwise shadow `scala.List`.
- `client/.../components/MuiExtensions.scala` fills gaps in the generated facades: `sxStyle` for `sx`, `target` on
  `SelectChangeEvent`, DOM events on `TextField`. Add helpers there instead of casting inline.
- ScalablyTyped builders become elements with `.build`. Do that when putting them in a `Seq` (e.g. `MenuItem`s,
  table rows); single children convert implicitly.
- Close `Dialog`s with `.onDismiss(callback)` from `MuiExtensions` (the generated `onClose` takes `js.Any`).
- Server calls go through `{{ class_prefix }}ClientRepository` (`.modelObjectOps.search(...)`, etc.), which maps the
  generated GraphQL types to the shared model.
- The theme lives in one place: `client/.../Theme.scala`.
- To add an npm library: add it to `stLib/package.json`, regenerate the facades (see Commands), and add it to
  `client/package.json`.
- The client calls the server with relative URLs (same origin). Don't hardcode hosts or ports.
{%- endif %}

## Updating from the template

The working tree must be clean. Then run `copier update --trust --defaults`, resolve any conflict markers, and run
the tests. To switch a variant (e.g. the database), run `copier update --trust --defaults --data database=postgres`
and expect to port any code you wrote against the old variant.
